// Vitest Snapshot v1, https://vitest.dev/guide/snapshot.html

exports[`canonFor > renders stable, compact prompt text 1`] = `
"# Canon canon-1ad1af4f8ce4b3 · ring/halo/0e1e868e5c73a7
## Rules (typical [band]; warnings give the reason)
- halo.clawWire (setting.prongDiameter) 0.8 [0.8–1.1]: Claws inside a halo
- halo.outline (haloOutline) 1.5 [1.4–1.6]: A single halo should take the face to 1.4–1.6× the centre; past 1.7× it reads as a tyre, not a frame.
- halo.melee (halo.stoneWidth) 1.2 [1.05–1.35]: Halo melee grows slowly with the centre: 1.1 mm round 5 mm, 1.25 round 6.5, 1.4 round 8. Bigger melee competes with the centre.
- halo.offset (halo.offset) 0.35 [0.35–0.6]: The halo must clear the claws (wire − 0.5, at least 0.3 mm) or bezel; wider than 0.6 mm it floats loose.
- halo.drop (halo.drop) 0.4 [0.3–0.6]: Seat the halo 0.3–0.6 mm under the girdle so the centre stands proud; never above it.
- halo.rows (halo.rows) 1 [1–2]: A second halo only round a centre of 6 mm or more; on smaller stones it buries them.
- ring.solitaire.bandWidth (ringShank.widthTop) 1.86 [1.5–2.79]: The band frames the stone: under 1.8 mm reads fragile and bends, over 2.4 mm on a 1 ct swamps it. Delicate needs W ≤ 6.5.
- ring.solitaire.narrowBandDepth (ringShank.thicknessTop) 1.85 [1.75–2.2]: A band under 2 mm wide must be nearly as deep as it is wide, or the head levers it out of round.
- ring.solitaire.elevation (gem.placement.elevation) 2.35 [0.8–2.65]: Classic sits 0.2–0.8 mm above the band; low (0.8–1.2) only in a basket or bezel; above 4 mm it catches on everything.
- ring.solitaire.tableHeight (tableHeight) 6.38 [4.53–8.04]: Height is what the hand feels: 6–7 mm for a classic 1 ct, 7 for everyday, 10.5 only for a cocktail ring.
- ring.head.prongWire (setting.prongDiameter) 0.95 [0.85–1.19]: Claws scale with the stone: 0.85 mm on 5 mm, 1.0 on 6.5, 1.15 on 8. Fatter looks clumsy; thinner wears through.
- ring.head.basketRails (setting.rails) 1 [1–2]: Claws longer than three wire-widths need a rail to brace them; two rails above about 5 mm of free claw.
- ring.head.pointsCovered (setting.prongCount) 4 [3–4]: A point is where a stone chips: each needs its own V-claw, so never fewer claws than points.
- stones.oneHero (secondStone) 4.34 [0–5.27]: One stone must win in a blink: the second-largest at most 0.85 of the hero, or the eye cannot settle.
- ring.solitaire.widthBottom (ringShank.widthBottom) 1.65 [1.5–1.85]: Band width at the base
- ring.solitaire.thicknessTop (ringShank.thicknessTop) 1.8 [1.7–1.9]: Band thickness under the head
- stones.softInPave (softestInPave) 10 [8–10]: Flush, channel, bar and pavé stones take the knocks face-on: only Mohs 8 and harder survive them.
- metals.colours (metalColours) 1 [1–2]: Unity: one or two metal colours. Three reads as three pieces fighting.
- metals.finishes (finishCount) 1 [1–2]: Unity: at most two finishes, used to separate planes; more is noise.
- rows.coverage (gemRow.coverage) 160 [120–270]: Coverage of a part row
- ring.head.prongCoverage (prongCoverage) 0.2 [0.1–0.35]: Metal over the girdle
- ring.head.taper (setting.taper) 0.25 [0.2–0.3]: Head taper
- ring.scale.headToFinger (headToFinger) 0.55 [0.3–0.8]: Head to finger
- ring.solitaire.thicknessBottom (ringShank.thicknessBottom) 1.6 [1.5–1.8]: Band thickness at the base
- stones.meleeColour (meleeColourGap) 1 [0–2]: Melee colour against the centre
- metals.tipFamily (tipFamilies) 1 [1–1]: One claw-tip family
- iced.levels (levelStep) 1.2 [0.8–1.5]: Steps between levels
- ring.head.prongsForShape (setting.prongCount) 4 [4–4]: Claws for the shape
- ring.base.euro (euroBase) 0 [0–1]: Euro base for big heads
- stones.lw (gem.lw) 1.05 [1–1.25]: Length to width
- stones.whiteHead (colourlessInYellowHead) 0 [0–0]: White head for colourless stones
- ring.scale.stoneToFinger (stoneToFinger) 0.42 [0.3–0.65]: Stone to finger
## Exemplars
- halo-cushion-classic, Cushion halo with pavé shoulders (halo, classic; classic): metal au18k-white, cut cushion, head basket, W 6.1, haloD 1.2, haloOffset 0.35, bandW 2.2, e 2.3, wire 0.85
  Why: Halo melee 1.2 mm on a 6.1 mm centre takes the face to 1.6×, a frame not a tyre. The shoulder pavé matches the halo size, so the two zones read as one. The halo clears the claws by the claw wire less 0.5 mm. Bad twin: Halo jammed into the claws.
- halo-oval-delicate, Delicate oval halo (halo, delicate; delicate): metal au14k-rose, cut oval, head basket, W 5.54, haloD 1.2, haloOffset 0.3, bandW 1.65, e 2.2, wire 0.8
  Why: A 1.65 mm band under a 5.5 mm oval: delicate, and the halo makes it read larger. Fine 0.8 mm claws inside the halo; the halo guards the girdle. Outline 1.6× the centre: below the 1.8× limit for small stones. Bad twin: Halo melee competing with the centre.
- halo-double-maximalist, Double halo cocktail ring (halo, bold; maximalist): metal pt950, cut cushion, head basket, W 10, haloD 1.6, haloOffset 0.6, bandW 5, e 2.5, wire 1.1
  Why: A 10 mm centre on a 5 mm band that tapers to a 2.5 × 1.8 mm euro base: scale held steady. Two rows of 1.6 mm melee stop at 1.85× the centre. Claws of 1.1 mm inside the halo keep its gap at 0.6 mm: no moat round the centre. Bad twin: A 2 mm band under a 10 mm stone.
## Cannot build: say so, offer the stand-in
- Hidden halo → tables face up, so a deep drop makes a skirt: build a visible halo and record a maker note
- French and U-cut pavé → build shared prong and say the setter will cut it
- Half-bezel arc other than 110° → the arc is fixed at 110° a side: a wider or narrower grip is a maker note
- Antique and fancy cuts → old European, rose, polki, tapered baguette, trapezoid and half-moon are not cuts here: name the stand-in
- Alloys and finishes we cannot specify → 21k, enamel, per-region plating and hand engraving: pick the nearest alloy and note the rest
## Watch for
- A "tyre" halo wider than 1.7× the centre
- A stone row running into the head
- Fat claws that hide the stone
- A "lollipop" head: a big head on a stick of a shank
- Band and stone out of scale with each other
## Ask (at most three)
- Metal, and pavé on the band or plain?"
`;

exports[`canonFor > renders stable, compact prompt text 2`] = `
"# Canon canon-1ad1af4f8ce4b3 · pendant/icedPendant/181efeaa597c71
## Rules (typical [band]; warnings give the reason)
- iced.thickness (vectorBody.thickness) 3.26 [2.5–4.07]: Iced pieces need depth for seats and presence: about 1.5 + 0.055 × height, 3.2 mm at 30, 5.6 at 75.
- iced.sizeMax (pave.sizeMax) 1 [0.8–1.3]: The largest stone is the bulk of the ice: 2 % of the height, 1.0–1.2 on a micro piece, 1.5–2.0 on XL. Big melee looks coarse.
- iced.metalUnder (metalUnderPave) 1.85 [1.45–3.25]: Under the recessed field keep 0.65 × stone + 0.8 mm of metal; thinner and the kernel quietly drops stones.
- iced.edge (paveEdge) 0.9 [0.5–2.5]: Rim plus margin at least 0.5 mm, never under 0.4: the outer stones need metal to be set against.
- iced.height (bodyHeight) 51 [25–102]: Micro 25–38 mm, small 38–64, mid 64–76, standard 76–102: an iced piece left at 22 mm is a charm.
- iced.gap (pave.gap) 0.2 [0.2–0.25]: Gap between pavé stones
- iced.coverage (pave.coverage) 1 [1–1]: Ice level
- bail.opening (bail.opening) 6.9 [6.6–8.4]: The hole must pass the chain section with 1.5 mm to spare, as the kernel measures it: a round chain needs link + 1.7 in a ring, a loop about twice the link.
- bail.wire (bail.wire) 1.38 [1–2.5]: A bail takes the whole weight on one bend: 1.1 mm on a small piece, about 1 + 0.012 × height on heavy ones (2.0 at 80 mm).
- iced.spread (pave.sizeMin) 0.8 [0.8–0.8]: Size spread
- iced.rim (vectorBody.border) 0.85 [0.7–1]: Polished rim
- iced.levels (levelStep) 1.2 [0.8–1.5]: Steps between levels
- stones.softInPave (softestInPave) 10 [8–10]: Flush, channel, bar and pavé stones take the knocks face-on: only Mohs 8 and harder survive them.
- metals.colours (metalColours) 1 [1–2]: Unity: one or two metal colours. Three reads as three pieces fighting.
- metals.finishes (finishCount) 1 [1–2]: Unity: at most two finishes, used to separate planes; more is noise.
- chain.cubanToPendant (chain.linkWidth) 3.84 [2.56–5.12]: Cuban width for the pendant
- chain.massToPendant (chainToPendantMass) 1 [0.5–1.5]: Chain weight against the pendant
- iced.margin (pave.margin) 0.4 [0.4–0.5]: Margin inside a rim
- chain.cuban.wire (chain.wire) 1.04 [0.88–1.2]: Cuban wire
- iced.envelope (bodyHeight) 51 [0–70]: Caster envelope
- chain.mensLength (chain.length) 559 [508–762]: Men's chain length
- stones.meleeColour (meleeColourGap) 1 [0–2]: Melee colour against the centre
- metals.tipFamily (tipFamilies) 1 [1–1]: One claw-tip family
- bail.width (bail.width) 3.45 [2.5–5.18]: Bail width
## Style: Iced out
Reads right: Scale first: 50 mm and up reads across a room; 25 mm is the floor. Fully flooded fields (ice level 1) inside a polished rim that draws the silhouette. Depth: 2–4 stacked levels 0.8–1.5 mm apart, each fully iced. A chain heavy enough to carry it: Cuban 8–16 % of the pendant height. Small, even melee: 1.0–1.5 mm reads as ice; 2 mm stones read as gravel.
Do: Ask artwork, height, ice level, stone tier and chain before anything else. Flood every field; build accents and two-tone as separate nodes. Give script one or two clean rows of stones (2.0 / 3.3 mm stroke at 1.3 mm stones). Use rhodium-plated white gold for "all ice"; yellow gold with white stones is classic too.
Don't: Leave a hip-hop piece at default scale (22 mm). Partial ice levels on a field. Union levels of different tones. Hang 50 mm+ iced pieces on a Figaro.
- vectorBody.thickness 1.5 + (0.055·H) [0.75·(1.5 + (0.055·H))–1.25·(1.5 + (0.055·H))]
- pave.sizeMax melee(clamp(0.02·H, 1, 2)) [1–2]
- pave.coverage 1 [1–1]: accents are a separate pavé node
- vectorBody.border clamp(0.85·paveMax, 0.6, 3) [0.6–3]
- chain.linkWidth 0.12·H [0.08·H–0.16·H]: Cuban or curb
## Exemplars
- iced-micro-initial, Iced initial on a tennis chain (icedPendant, delicate; iced out, hip hop): metal au14k-white, H 32, T 3.25, paveMax 1, border 0.85, chainW 3.4, opening 10.85
  Why: Micro tier: 1.0 mm melee reads as ice at 32 mm. A 0.85 mm polished rim draws the letter. A hidden bail behind the ice, its slot sized for the 3.4 mm tennis links as the kernel measures it. Bad twin: Coarse stones on a small piece.
- iced-sculpted-trophy, Flooded trophy sculpt (icedPendant, classic; iced out, hip hop): metal au14k-yellow, H 45, paveMax 1, chainW 6, opening 9.2
  Why: Hollowed to a 1.5 mm wall and sliced half flat: about 50 g of body, not a 136 g solid lump. Every face flooded with 1.0 mm stones kept 0.6 mm in from each edge. A 6 mm Cuban carries 45 mm on a hidden bail behind the ice. Bad twin: A half-iced field with bare edges.
- iced-cross, Iced cross on a Figaro (icedPendant, classic; iced out, hip hop): metal au14k-white, H 45, T 4, paveMax 1, border 0.85, chainW 4, opening 6.9
  Why: A 45 mm cross is the most a 4 mm Figaro should carry. All ice: rhodium-plated white gold throughout. Bad twin: A 60 mm iced cross on a Figaro.
## Cannot build: say so, offer the stand-in
- Scooped back → an outline body cannot be scooped: quote the solid mass and note that the maker may scoop
- Pavé on chain links → a plain Cuban with a maker note, or a tennis chain instead
- Pavé density and partial rows on strokes → the kernel does not report iced area yet: compare stone count with 100/(0.866·(d + gap)²) per cm² by hand; on script, one or two clean rows
- per-region plating → separate nodes per tone
- franco, herringbone, snake, paperclip chains → nearest of cable, curb, Cuban, Figaro, rope, box, tennis
- Half-bezel arc other than 110° → the arc is fixed at 110° a side: a wider or narrower grip is a maker note
## Watch for
- Default scale: a hip-hop pendant left at 22 mm
- Illegible iced text: strokes too thin for a clean row
- An under-built bail on a heavy piece
- Ragged pavé: bald patches and uneven row ends
- A front-only design: the profile and back never considered
## Ask (at most three)
- Artwork and height?
- Ice level and stone tier: natural, lab or moissanite?
- Chain style, width and length?"
`;

